  "exploiter_output": {
    "evidence": "Root cause: net/sf/mpxj/common/InputStreamHelper.java:126, `processZipStream(File dir, InputStream inputStream)` \u2014 `File file = new File(dir, entry.getName());` builds the destination path directly from the ZIP entry name (an untrusted value crossing the trust boundary at the point an attacker-supplied/attacker-influenced archive is fed into InputStreamHelper.writeZipStreamToTempDir(InputStream), a public API with no caller-side validation) with no canonical-path containment check. Verified live: reproduced on current code (exit 0) and reproduced-fixed against a representative canonical-path guard inserted at InputStreamHelper.java:126-130 (exit 1), then reverted (git status confirms zero product diff remains).\n\nEvery sink fed by that same unsanitized `file`/`parent` must be covered by a complete fix \u2014 all reachable from the identical entry.getName() taint source in this one method:\n1. InputStreamHelper.java:139 \u2014 `new FileOutputStream(file)` \u2014 direct file write for a traversal entry name (the path this POV exercises).\n2. InputStreamHelper.java:129 \u2192 FileHelper.java:95/99 (`FileHelper.mkdirsQuietly(file)`, sink `file.mkdirs()`) \u2014 directory-entry case (`entry.isDirectory()`), where a \"../\"-laden directory entry name causes directory creation outside `dir`.\n3. InputStreamHelper.java:133/136 \u2192 FileHelper.java:95/99 (`FileHelper.mkdirsQuietly(parent)`, sink `file.mkdirs()`) \u2014 the parent-directory creation for a non-directory entry, reachable even for entries whose immediate name is not itself the escape point but whose parent path (via nested \"../\" segments) is.\n\nAll three sinks derive from the single `File file = new File(dir, entry.getName())` at line 126, so a fix at that one construction point (validate/normalize before use) covers all three; a fix applied only at the FileOutputStream call site (sink 1) would leave sinks 2 and 3 exploitable via directory-only entries.",
    "expected_vulnerable_behavior": "On unpatched code the test passes (JUnitCore exits 0): InputStreamHelper.writeZipStreamToTempDir() extracts the malicious zip entry \"../pov-zipslip-marker-<n>.txt\" to a file located in the parent of the returned temp directory (i.e. outside the sandboxed extraction dir), containing the attacker-supplied payload \"zip-slip-proof-of-vulnerability\". After a correct fix (rejecting/normalizing traversal entries or containing them inside `dir`), the escaped file is never created and the test fails (non-zero exit), either via an explicit IOException from the guard or via the assertion `escapedFile.exists()` failing.",
    "notes": "Targeted alert group's shared root-cause trace: line 126 (`new File(dir, entry.getName())`) feeding into the FileOutputStream write sink at InputStreamHelper.java:139 (the alert's third vulnerability group, single-trace). I chose this as the primary POV because it is the most direct sink from the taint source (no intermediate mkdirsQuietly indirection) and the resulting artifact \u2014 a written file with attacker-controlled content outside the sandboxed temp dir \u2014 is the clearest, most unambiguous proof of exploitability (arbitrary file write, not merely arbitrary directory creation). The other two alert traces (vulnerability group 1: line 126\u2192129\u2192FileHelper.java:95/99 for directory entries, and line 126\u2192133\u2192136\u2192FileHelper.java:95/99 for parent-directory creation of file entries) share the identical unguarded construction at line 126 and are documented in `evidence`/`regression_hint` so a patch that only special-cases the FileOutputStream call site (and misses the two mkdirsQuietly call sites triggered by directory entries or nested parent paths) will be flagged as incomplete.\n\nVerified end-to-end inside the project container using the provided Docker wrapper: `mvn -B -DskipTests package` to produce target/classes, then javac+java (JUnitCore) against target/classes + the project's bundled junit/hamcrest jars in lib/. Confirmed pov_command exits 0 on the untouched vulnerable source, and exits 1 when a representative canonical-path containment guard is temporarily inserted at InputStreamHelper.java:126-130 (then fully reverted \u2014 `git status --porcelain` shows only the untracked `.security-pipeline/` POV directory, zero product diff).",
    "pov_command": "mkdir -p .security-pipeline/pov/out && javac -cp target/classes:lib/junit-4.13.1.jar:lib/hamcrest-core-1.3.jar -d .security-pipeline/pov/out .security-pipeline/pov/ZipSlipPovTest.java && java -cp .security-pipeline/pov/out:target/classes:lib/junit-4.13.1.jar:lib/hamcrest-core-1.3.jar org.junit.runner.JUnitCore net.sf.mpxj.common.ZipSlipPovTest",
    "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/ZipSlipPovTest.java",
    "regression_hint": "Invariant: for every ZipEntry extracted in InputStreamHelper.processZipStream, the resolved destination (both the entry's own File and, for non-directory entries, its parent File) must have a canonical path that is equal to, or a strict descendant of, the canonical path of the temp extraction `dir` returned by writeZipStreamToTempDir \u2014 i.e. `file.getCanonicalPath().startsWith(dir.getCanonicalPath() + File.separator)` (or the file being extracted equals dir itself, though that's not a realistic case). Any entry that fails this check must be rejected (throw, or skip the entry) before any of mkdirsQuietly(file) [line 129], mkdirsQuietly(parent) [line 136], or `new FileOutputStream(file)` [line 139] is invoked \u2014 checking only one of the three sinks is insufficient since directory entries and file entries take different branches from the same tainted `file`/`parent` values.\n\nKnown bypass variants the fix must also block, not just simple leading \"../\":\n- Nested/mid-path traversal: \"sub/../../evil.txt\" (parent-directory computation at line 133/136 still escapes even if the literal entry name doesn't start with \"../\").\n- Directory-entry traversal: a ZipEntry with isDirectory()==true and a \"../\"-laden name, exercised only via the mkdirsQuietly(file) sink at line 129 (never reaches FileOutputStream, so a fix guarding only the file-write branch misses this).\n- Absolute path entry names (e.g. \"/etc/cron.d/evil\" or Windows \"C:\\\\evil\"), where `new File(dir, entry.getName())` \u2014 because Java's File 2-arg constructor treats an absolute second argument as replacing `dir` entirely on some scenarios/platforms \u2014 can resolve completely outside `dir` regardless of \"..\" segments.\n- Backslash-encoded traversal on platforms/JVMs that treat \"\\\\\" as a separator, or URL/percent-encoded segments if any decoding occurs upstream of this method.\n- Symlink-based escapes: even a name that stays lexically under `dir` could still escape if an intermediate path component is a symlink created by an earlier entry in the same archive \u2014 canonicalization (getCanonicalPath, not just getAbsolutePath/normalize) is required to catch this, and ordering-dependent symlink-then-traverse sequences within one archive should be considered.",
    "status": "pov_created",
    "summary": "Created a JUnit4 PoV (ZipSlipPovTest) that feeds a malicious zip through the real public API InputStreamHelper.writeZipStreamToTempDir(InputStream). The zip's only entry is named \"../pov-zipslip-marker-<n>.txt\". processZipStream() builds `new File(dir, entry.getName())` (InputStreamHelper.java:126) without validating the resolved path stays under `dir`, so the payload is written one directory level above the returned temp extraction directory \u2014 a classic Zip Slip / CWE-22 path traversal. The test asserts the escaped file exists outside `dir` with the attacker's content, proving arbitrary-location file write via untrusted archive input."
  },
